@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — two generalizations from the caution layer, read against
# each other
#
# They are in one file because they are the two halves of one question and
# were formed in the same pass. A transmission that claims something states
# conditions; the question this holding has to answer of every such condition
# is whether anybody could check it. The first statement collects the
# conditions that put the claim out of reach of a check, the second the
# conditions that do not — and the second is the smaller and the more useful
# set, because it is the only place where a protocol has something to control
# for.
#
# Both were formed after the counter-search against this holding and not
# before it, which is what SPEC §16 requires and what prima-materia#667 was
# opened for. Both counter-searches found something, and what they found
# stands in the notes rather than in a later correction.
#

pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Where the capacity is in a person, the deliberate test of it is forbidden"@en, "Wo das Vermögen in einer Person liegt, ist seine absichtliche Prüfung verboten"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission locates a claimed capacity in a person rather than in a procedure that anyone could work, it states a rule withdrawing that person from a deliberate test, and it grounds the rule in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it — that the undivided attention it requires is destroyed by the testing, that the party addressed withdraws from a question put as a test, that no material sign it could produce would settle anything — and not in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. The rule governs a test proposed by an enquirer or an onlooker. Where a transmission instead has two practitioners measure their capacities against one another, it fixes a criterion and does not forbid the trial."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstProvingTheLucidity, pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, pmc:SigneMaterielCaution ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses say it in three idioms and the ground is a property of the thing claimed in each. The magnetic manual makes it a general rule from which the operator is never to depart, never on any account to permit any attempt to prove the somnambulist's lucidity, and derives the prohibition in the same chapter from the prerequisite that lucidity requires an undivided attention: the reason the faculty cannot be tested is the reason it is said to work. The spiritist manual states that superior spirits have no sign by which to be recognised other than the superiority of their ideas, that every spirit can imitate a material sign and that the names of saints are taken freely, so that neither a sign nor a name is proof. The Pali discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, holds only the third to be reliable, and refuses the display of power for the sake of persuading anyone as something to be ashamed of."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not the claim at pmc:TestingTheProcedureVoidsItsAnswer and the difference is the antecedent. That one is about transmissions that obtain an answer from a procedure — a cast, a consultation — and says the repeated or testing question is excluded. This one is about transmissions whose claim is a capacity of a named person, where there is no procedure a stranger could work and therefore nothing to repeat. A transmission can satisfy either without the other, and the spiritist manual happens to satisfy both, in two different chapters and for two different reasons."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was run against this holding before the statement was written, and it found one case that bounds it. Among the Sámi material, when two practitioners wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted two reindeer striking each other on a drum and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better. That is a deliberate trial of a capacity located in a person, with a criterion, an outcome that could fall either way and no party controlling it — and it is prescribed rather than forbidden. What distinguishes it from the three witnesses is who proposes the test: there it is the practitioners themselves and the loser remains a practitioner, whereas the rule in all three witnesses answers an enquirer or an onlooker who wants to be convinced. The last sentence of the statement carries that boundary, and a second case of the same shape would strengthen the statement rather than break it."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the three witnesses have a documented route between them: the spiritist manual of 1861 comes out of the magnetic milieu of the preceding century and its author knew that literature. They are therefore not two independent witnesses, and no independence is claimed here — a generalization asserts a recurring form and not a count of witnesses. The Pali discourse is unconnected to both, and it is the witness that decides whether the form is older than the nineteenth century."@en ;
    skos:note "What the statement costs the claims it is about is worth saying plainly, because it is the reason to hold it. A rule of this shape makes every negative result attributable to the testing, and one of the three witnesses states that consequence itself: where an incredulous person consults in order to put the somnambulist to the proof, he is said to exert, without knowing it, an influence that furnishes him with new reasons for his incredulity. That is a prediction that a failed test confirms the claim, and it is the point at which a transmission stops being examinable from outside."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission claiming a capacity of a person whose earliest witness invites an outsider to test it, states what a negative outcome would be, and treats such an outcome as telling against the capacity rather than against the conditions of the trial. The nineteenth-century literature on both sides of the magnetic dispute is where such a case would most likely be found, and it has not been opened in this holding."@en ;
    skos:note "Not yet examined, and the queue of SPEC §16 is where this belongs. No pm:Testing points at it."@en .

pmc:TheBodyOfThePartiesIsAStatedCondition a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The rules addressed to the operator begin with what he has eaten and how he feels"@en, "Die Regeln an den Ausführenden beginnen bei dem, was er gegessen hat, und wie ihm zumute ist"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes an operation on a body, whether the operator's own or another's, and addresses any rule at all to the persons taking part in it, those rules include states of recent intake and of strong feeling — drink, a full stomach, hunger, thirst, exhaustion, anger, fright — under which the operation is not to be performed. The condition is stated of a person present, and not of the day, the place or the material. Where such a transmission states what follows from proceeding anyway, what it states is a disorder of the body and not a failure of the operation."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:WucijinQizhuang, pmc:XingqiProhibitions, pmc:ArsNotoriaEbrietasKautel, pmc:CautionAgainstOperatingAfterEating ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "Four transmissions in four corpora with no route among them. The Chinese medical canon names seven states in which the needle is not to be used: greatly drunk, greatly angered, greatly exhausted, newly filled with food, greatly hungry, greatly thirsty, greatly startled. The nourishing-life chapter of the Baopuzi forbids eating much and forbids anger to the man circulating his breath. The Ars Notoria warns that no man presume to say the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without reverence and discretion. The magnetic manual instructs that one is not to operate after having eaten heartily nor during digestion, and that whoever undertakes a treatment ought in general to live temperately."@en ;
    skos:note "Which person the condition is stated of varies and the statement is worded to carry the variation. In the medical canon it is the person needled; in the nourishing-life chapter and in the Latin oration it is the practitioner working on himself; in the magnetic manual it is the operator working on another. What does not vary is that the condition attaches to a body in the room."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the class of condition a protocol can control for, and that is the whole reason to hold the statement. The other conditions this holding carries in quantity — a defect of the operator nobody noticed, a state of faith, the sympathy of the bystanders, a trial set at what the candidate's mind does not grasp — cannot be established before the operation or checked after it. Time since the last meal, quantity of drink and a reported state of anger or fright can be. A run that wanted to build an examination of any of these four transmissions would have here the only entry the sources themselves supply."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the part that can fail and is therefore the part worth having. Two of the four state a consequence and both state it as a disorder of the body: needling the greatly drunk makes the qi disordered, needling the greatly angered makes the qi go against its course, and much anger disorders the qi so that it cannot flow over and may set a man coughing. The other two state no consequence at all. A transmission of this kind whose penalty for the full stomach is that the operation does not come off, rather than that the practitioner is harmed, would break the clause without touching the rest."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was run against this holding before the statement was written. It found one transmission that prescribes an operation on a body and names no such condition: the canonical Mesopotamian incantation series, in which every prohibition read is addressed to the demon and not to the operator, and in which what the operator must do and must not do is stated nowhere inside the series. That is why the antecedent carries the clause about addressing any rule at all to the persons taking part — the exorcistic series fails it, and the statement is about transmissions that have operator-facing rules in the first place. The other near miss is the whole class of calendar-bound conditions, which state the condition of the day rather than of a person and are generalized separately at pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that prescribes a bodily operation, carries rules for the persons performing it — purity, dress, posture, hour — and carries none about what they have eaten, drunk or felt. The grimoire literature is the place to look, because it is dense in operator rules of every other kind, and one such case refutes the statement."@en ;
    skos:note "Not yet examined, and no pm:Testing points at it. Of the two statements in this file it is the one an examination could actually address, because the condition it names is one an experiment can set."@en .
